<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonaïves</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonaïves is a port city on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artibonite">Artibonite</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. On 1 January 1804 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> proclaimed the independence of Haiti there, and it is known as the City of Independence. It has often been the starting point of Haitian uprisings, including the revolts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-duvalier">Jean-Claude Duvalier</a> in 1985 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bertrand-aristide">Jean-Bertrand Aristide</a> in 2004, and it was devastated by floods in 2004 and 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonaives.jpg" alt="Gonaïves" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernandolouverture · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cathedral_of_the_Souvenir_Gonaives.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gonaïves lies on a flood plain, below mountains that are largely unforested. Even among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>'s port cities it has long been vulnerable to hurricanes for this reason. The Gulf of Gonâve takes its name from the town, which is also called Les Gonaïves, and in Haitian Creole Gonayiv.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was founded around 1422 by a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, who named it Gonaibo, a locality of the cacicat of the Jaragua. In 1802 the Battle of Ravine-à-Couleuvres, an important engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, was fought near it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 1 January 1804 by reading the Act of Independence, drafted by Boisrond Tonnerre, on the Place d'Armes of the town. His wife, Marie-Claire Heureuse Félicité, died in Gonaïves in August 1858.</p><p>In the early 2000s the city saw substantial rioting and violence, motivated mainly by opposition to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bertrand-aristide">Jean-Bertrand Aristide</a>. On 5 February 2004 a group calling itself the Revolutionary Artibonite Resistance Front seized control of the city, which began the 2004 Haitian rebellion.</p><p>Storms struck the city twice in four years. In September 2004 Hurricane Jeanne caused major flooding and mudslides. Four years later Hurricane Hanna devastated it again and killed 529 people, mostly in the flooded parts of Gonaïves, where the destruction was described as catastrophic. At least 48,000 people from the Gonaïves area were forced into shelters, according to the mayor, Stephen Moise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gonaïves is a commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artibonite">Artibonite</a> department. Its population was 356,324 at the 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The city has a public university, the Université Publique de l'Artibonite aux Gonaïves, as well as a law and economics school and La Providence Hospital. Its association football clubs include Eclair AC and Racing FC.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gonaïves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jean-Jacques Dessalines proclaimed the independence of Haiti at <b>Gonaïves</b> after more than a decade of revolution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating Napoleon's army at Vertières in November 1803, Jean-Jacques Dessalines proclaimed the independence of Haiti at <b>Gonaïves</b> on 1 January 1804, reviving the island's Taíno name.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">First Empire of Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
